00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.710 align:start position:0%
a professor at the university of  maryland asked this awesomely evil test  question  you have the opportunity to earn extra  points for this exam select whether you  want two points or six points but there  is a small catch  if more than ten 10 of the class picks  six points then no one will get any  points  only i will see your selection it will  be anonymous to the rest of the class  do you pick two points or six points  when this question was posted to twitter  it went viral with a lot of people  discussing the question it looks like  there's a dilemma here  and a lot of people thought that look if  we at least picks two points if everyone  can cooperate then at least we can get  two  but that's not the game theory way to  think about this question so let's try  and analyze what the correct strategic  decision should be given the incentives  of this question  you want to think about your choice  relative to what the other people in the  class are doing and the relevant  characteristic is how many people are  picking six  it could be that more than ten percent  of the classes picking six  it could be the case that fewer than ten  percent of the class is picking six or  there's a small probability that maybe  exactly ten percent of the other people  are picking six  we'll construct a matrix based on what  the other people are doing  to see what your payoff will be  if more than 10 percent of the class is  picking six  then it doesn't matter whether you pick  two or six everyone is going to get zero  points  now if fewer than 10 percent of the  other people are picking six say  everyone else is picking two  then you are going to get the number of  points that you pick if you pick two  points you'll get two if you pick six  points you'll get six  and now you can see something very  interesting  you are always better off at least as  good off picking six as if you are  picking two  so picking six here is a logical choice  whether the class is more than ten  percent picking six or less than ten  percent picking six  you are always going to score more  points if you pick six versus two  now there's one third case to consider  is that maybe exactly ten percent pick  six  in this case you would get two if you  pick two  but you would ruin it for everyone and  get zero if you pick six you would  you know increase the threshold beyond  10  so in this one small scenario you're  actually better off picking two points  but it's kind of rare and there's no way  you could know that exactly 10 percent  of the class is picking six so in a  class of 10 that would be exactly one  person picking six and everyone else  picking two this is a very unlikely  scenario  so you can kind of disregard this third  possibility and you could say picking  six is a logical thing to do  but there's another reason to think  about why picking six is logical  exams are not usually based on the  absolute number of points you get it's  not usually a point threshold that  ninety percent is an a eighty percents  of b that's usually true in grade school  or high school but once you get to  college everything is usually graded on  a curve  your grade is based on how much better  you do relative to everyone else  so you don't want to think about the  absolute number of points you're getting  you want to think about how many more  points you're getting than other people  so when you think in this relativistic  method  if you get two and everyone else gets  two you're getting zero extra points  whereas if you get six and other people  get two then you have a chance to get  four extra points  so we want to reduce the payouts when  points are awarded by two  so if the class is getting more than ten  percent picking six no one's getting  points anyway  if fewer than ten percent are picking  six  then when you pick two if everyone gets  two  you're not getting any more points than  anyone else who's picking two  but if you get six and other people are  getting two you get four extra points  and then look at the third scenario  where exactly ten percent is picking six  so say one in ten  out of your ten percent class is picking  six  and one person's picking six everyone  else is picking two  now if you pick two then you're not  going to do any better than the people  who are picking two and if you pick six  and spoil it for everyone then you get  zero  so when you look at the payouts now  picking six is always at least as good  as picking two  so in this case picking six is the  logical choice and it's what's known as  a weekly dominant strategy so when you  have a weekly dominant strategy you're  going to want to play it  now the unfortunate outcome even though  everyone individually is thinking about  picking six and they all pick six you  end up in a situation that everyone ends  up with zero points but this is not  because any individual person is  thinking wrong this game is sort of  rigged so that the group outcome sort of  says oh you pick six you're selfish but  that's not really the case i think it  should be your decision to pick six and  that's the logical thing to do  thanks for watching this video  if you like more videos about game  theory and math please subscribe to my  channel  you can catch me on my blog mind your  decisions which you can follow on  facebook google plus and patreon you can  also catch me on social media at  preshtalwalker and if you like this  video please check out my books i've  written a book about game theory an  introduction to strategic thinking which  will help you understand this example  and many more ways to use math to  outsmart your opponents
 

